For each of the following four three-dimensional autonomous quadratic ODE systems on $\mathbb{R}^3$, where $A\in\mathbb{R}$ is a real parameter, give a rigorous proof of the existence of a chaotic solution (e.g., the existence of an invariant set on which the flow is topologically conjugate, or semi-conjugate, to a shift on a finite alphabet, exhibiting positive topological entropy of Rössler-attractor type):

(i) $x' = y^2 - x + Az,\quad y' = x,\quad z' = y$;

(ii) $x' = yz - x + Ay,\quad y' = z,\quad z' = x$;

(iii) $x' = yz + Az,\quad y' = x - y,\quad z' = y$;

(iv) $x' = y^2 + Az,\quad y' = x - y,\quad z' = y$.

Numerical evidence indicates that, for suitable values of $A$, each system possesses a Rössler-type strange attractor. The task is to produce a rigorous (e.g., computer-assisted topological or analytic) proof of chaos for each of the four systems.

